===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|xno|en}} legal phrase {{m|fro|hu e cri}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-hue and cry.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+|hues and cries}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} The [[public]] [[pursuit]] of a [[criminal]], accompanied by [[shout]]s to warn others to [[give chase]].
#* '''1797''', Richard Burn, John Burn (corrections and updates), ''The Justice of the Peace, and Parish Officer'', Volume II, 18th Edition, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=8JEDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA753&dq=%22hue+and+cry%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=BGuUUa2BI46aiQeKqYD4Bw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hue%20and%20cry%22&f=false page 753]:
#*: Because he that firſt raiſeth a '''hue and cry''', where no felony is committed, that is, he who giveth the falſe information, is ſeverely puniſhable by fine and impriſonment, if the information be falſe.
#* {{RQ:British Encyclopedia|volume=III|entry=HUE and CRY|noformat=yes|url=https://archive.org/details/gri_33125014817791/page/n548/mode/1up|column=1|passage=For the levying of '''hue and cry''', although it is a good course to have a justice's warrant, where time will permit, in order to prevent causeless '''hue and cry'''; yet it is not necessary, nor always convenient; for the felon may escape before the warrant be obtained.}}
#* '''1846''', Matthew Bacon, Sir Henry Gwilliam, Charles Edward Dodd, John Bouvier, ''A New Abridgment of the Law with Large Additions and Corrections'', Volume 4, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Zm8yAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA694&dq=%22hue+and+cry%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=BGuUUa2BI46aiQeKqYD4Bw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hue%20and%20cry%22&f=false page 693]:
#*: That in case of a '''hue and cry''' once raised and levied upon supposal of a felony committed, though in truth there was no felony committed; yet those, who pursue '''hue and cry''', may arrest and proceed as if a felony had been really committed.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[loud]] and [[persistent]] public [[clamour]], especially one associated with [[protest]] or the making of some [[demand]].
#* '''1955''', Caryl Chessman, ''Trial by Ordeal'', quoted in 1956 September, Walter P. Armstrong, Jr, book review, ''[[w:ABA Journal|American Bar Association Journal]]'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=0SxpgQ9PjgwC&pg=PA854&dq=%22hue+and+cry%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=BGuUUa2BI46aiQeKqYD4Bw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hue%20and%20cry%22&f=false page 854]:
#*: And because I haven't written off easily, a great '''hue and cry''' has gone up that there is something wrong with our whole system of administering justice.

